Regency Government Article 13 (1) Efforts undertaken by the Regency Government in order to prevent violations of Women's rights include:
a. conducting socialization regarding prevention of violence against Women by optimizing the role of customary, cultural, religious institutions and community organizations;
b. providing guidelines on handling acts of violence against Women;
c. involving community participation in recovery and reintegration; d. developing an integrated database and information system on acts of violence against Women;
e. developing family empowerment programs;
f. strengthening the function of community organizations engaged in Women's empowerment; and
g. providing public facilities that are comfortable and safe for Women.
(2) Efforts undertaken by the Regency Government in order to prevent violations of Children's rights include:
a. preventing acts of violence against Children by optimizing the role of customary, cultural, religious institutions and community organizations; b. developing child-friendly schools;
c. encouraging increased participation of children through the establishment of Children's Forums;
d. providing guidelines on handling acts of violence against Children;
e. involving community participation in recovery and reintegration services for child victims of violence;
f. developing an integrated database and information system on acts of violence against Children;
g. strengthening the function of community organizations engaged in Child protection; and
h. encouraging the establishment of Child-Friendly Regency.

Part Two
Rights of the Child Article 6 (1) Every Child has the right:
a. to live, grow, develop, and participate reasonably in accordance with human dignity and worth, and to receive protection from violence and discrimination;
b. to obtain a name as personal identity and citizenship status; c. to worship according to their religion, to think, and to express themselves in accordance with their level of intelligence and age under the guidance of Parents or Guardians; d. to obtain education and teaching in the context of personal development and their level of intelligence in accordance with their interests and talents and to receive protection in educational units from sexual crimes and Violence committed by educators, educational personnel, fellow students, and/or other parties;
e. to obtain protection from being targets of persecution, torture, or imposition of inhumane punishment;
f. to obtain freedom in accordance with the law and arrest, detention, or criminal imprisonment of a Child shall only be carried out if in accordance with applicable law and can only be carried out as a last resort;
g. to be cared for by their own Parents, unless there are reasons and/or valid legal rules showing that the separation is in the best interests of the Child and is a last consideration;
h. to obtain protection from abuse in political activities, involvement in armed conflict, involvement in social unrest, involvement in events containing elements of Violence, involvement in warfare and, sexual crimes;
i. to participate, express opinions and think in accordance with their conscience and religion;
j. to receive oral or written information in accordance with the Child's age stage and development;
k. to associate and assemble; and
l. to rest, play, recreate, create, and engage in cultural arts, to obtain play facilities that meet health and safety requirements and are adapted to the Child's age, the Child's level of ability, and their environment so as not to hinder and disturb. (2) Every child who is deprived of liberty has the right to:
a. receive humane treatment;
b. placement separated from adults;
c. obtain legal assistance or other assistance effectively at every stage of applicable legal remedies; and
d. defend themselves and obtain justice before an objective and impartial Children's court in a closed hearing to the public. (3) Every child who becomes a victim or perpetrator of sexual violence or who is in conflict with the law has the right to confidentiality.
(4) Every Child with Disabilities in addition to obtaining the rights as referred to in paragraph (1) is also entitled to obtain:
a. special education/special education;
b. rehabilitation;
c. social assistance; and
d. maintenance of social welfare standards.
(5) Every Child who has excellence has the right to receive special education.
